WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
The second pillar of this linguistic
architecture is ambiguity.

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.220
This occurs when expressions contain
multiple distinct meanings,

3
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.900
whether through lexical, structural, or
referential errors.

4
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.120
An argument becomes invalid if it subtly
shifts the meaning of an ambiguous

5
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.700
term between a premise and a conclusion, a
flaw known as the equivocation

6
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.520
fallacy. The third pillar, vagueness, is
structurally different.

7
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.140
It involves words with imprecise boundaries,
where it is unclear if an object

8
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.080
belongs in the term's extension.

9
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.620
Terms like tall or old lack exact
numerical cutoffs.

10
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.480
In poorly constructed research proposals,
vague language often hides errors in

11
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.180
the underlying moral reasoning.

12
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.720
Auditing for ambiguity and vagueness
effectively sanitizes the data before it

13
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.460
ever enters the formal logical deduction
phase of the review.
